Hi Alexandre, hi all,

Following up on my reply yesterday: I've completed the QSM port to the
shared per-VM D-Bus foundation and uploaded the source, patches and a
matching preview build:

https://github.com/rdmitry0911/qsm-rd/releases/tag/v0.4.0-qsm-direct-20261015-shared9

The original D-Bus patch retains Alexandre's authorship and sign-off.
qemu-server owns the bus and its lifetime; QSM attaches as a consumer.
The proposed opt-in is dbus-display: 1, with GPU type/rendernode in vga
and driver=dbus for audio0. CPU displays can keep VNC; GL displays cannot
provide VNC/SPICE alongside the D-Bus display.

The common display foundation, WebRTC API and pve-manager UI are separate
series. A small pve-guest-common module now handles the local console
service exchange for both VM and LXC APIs. Native signalling, status and
media settings use PVE's existing HTTPS origin on8006, with VM.Console
for offers/reads and VM.Config.Options for settings writes. There is no
separate HTTPS port; WebRTC media still uses UDP.

I checked Firefox video and an ordinary Reconnect in the embedded VM
console, and LXC video/settings, with the old8007 service stopped.
Anonymous reads and writes without a CSRF token are rejected. The Firefox
mDNS negotiation and reconnect issues found during VPN testing are fixed.
The earlier shared-base lab checks also covered CPU/GL display, audio,
microphone, clipboard and a second independent display consumer.

The graphical LXC sessions and provisioning remain in a separate add-on.
Their API patches can be reviewed independently of the QEMU foundation.
There are builds against pve-container6.1.13 and6.1.14, so the newer
container package does not need to be downgraded.

Markus Frank's Venus configuration adaptation and the NVIDIA readback
patch remain separate optional work. Runtime Venus and full Kyber/RDP
browser integration have not been qualified by these tests.

The source bases, package hashes, test logs and migration instructions
are here:
https://github.com/rdmitry0911/qsm-rd/tree/main/qsm-direct/upstream

I'd appreciate feedback on the VM-wide option, bus lifetime, audio
configuration and the API/service split. The patches are still a proposal;
I have not treated the new configuration as an agreed upstream interface.

Thanks,
Dmitry
